{"kind":"notes","id":"nt/pykj35","slideIndex":1,"text":"[2] CDC/NIOSH Pocket Guide to Chemical Hazards: Hydrogen sulfide. https://www.cdc.gov/niosh/npg/npgd0337.html Accessed 26 October 2026. IDLH is 100 ppm. NIOSH REL is a 10 ppm ceiling for a 10-minute period, not an 8-hour TWA. NIOSH states that the sense of smell rapidly fatigues and cannot be relied on to warn of continuous H2S presence. These U.S. references are context, not claims about applicable Indian legal limits.\n[3] Product role: https://github.com/aaryanadiger/VajraSetu/tree/57d48d1238d04b483a492bf38bbc212b745c98f2, README.md and docs/OPERATIONS_AND_SAFETY.md. A low band reading does not prove safe air. Certified/direct-reading monitors and site procedures remain essential. The prototype cannot identify instantaneous peaks."}
